{
  "abstract": "We study how many regular five-element subsets can occur in a rational Diophantine septuple. Assuming completeness of specified characteristic-zero polynomial-system classifications, we obtain a global upper bound of seven. The remaining computational qualification is stated explicitly: exact substitution and arithmetic checks certify the reported solutions, while msolve's multimodular stopping criterion supplies completeness. We combine the known five-edge theorem for degree-four graphs with the known $K_{2,3}$ obstruction and an elementary classification of connected subcubic eight-edge graphs on seven vertices. Only six theta configurations need exclusion, independently of the unfinished classification of seven-edge sources. Three are covered by earlier work. For the other three, we give a nine-leaf four-cycle calculation and two symmetric pair quotients. Their finite outputs are ruled out by explicit root-free reductions modulo five, the identity $f^2=21$, and a strictly positive quartic in an actual pair product. We also apply the previously known zero-pair matching lemma to show that the nonvanishing hypotheses of the four-cycle pivot equations are automatic; no separate zero-radicand chart stratification is needed. Exact verifiers check every equation in the reported finite factors. An independently verified lift gives all twenty-one square roots of a septuple over a totally real degree-eight field, as a nonrational existence control. The bound does not assert attainment over the rationals, classify every sparse graph, or resolve the rational septuple problem.",
